view formula-electrical-quantities | electrical-quantity | electric-charge,electric-current,potential-difference,electrical-power,electrical-energy-transfer | formula | Electrical Quantities | tables/formulas/electrical-quantities.md | These relations separate charge, current, potential difference, electrical power, and transferred energy. They require a system or component, terminal pair or oriented surface, time basis, and consistent voltage-current sign convention.
end-view
view formula-force | force | physical-interaction | formula | Force - Newtonian Net-Force Formula View | tables/formulas/force.md | In Newtonian mechanics, Newton's second law relates the net external force on a chosen system to its mass and acceleration. The familiar `F = ma` shorthand is useful only when `F` is understood as the vector sum of external forces and the system and model assumptions are fixed.
end-view
view formula-general-accounting-balance | work-energy-power | energy | formula | General Accounting Balance | tables/formulas/general-accounting-balance.md | An accounting balance tracks a countable property inside a chosen system over time. Input and generation add to the system; output and consumption remove from it. When the property is strictly conserved, generation and consumption are zero.
end-view
view formula-information-signal-measures | information-data-signal-noise | self-information,shannon-entropy,signal-to-noise-ratio | formula | Information and Signal Measures | tables/formulas/information-signal-measures.md | These relations quantify modeled surprise, average discrete uncertainty, and selected signal-to-noise comparisons. They do not measure semantic meaning, truth, usefulness, or universal signal quality.
end-view
view formula-kinematics-motion-measures | motion-measure | position,distance-traveled,displacement,speed,velocity,acceleration | formula | Kinematics Motion Measures | tables/formulas/kinematics-motion-measures.md | These relations separate path-based scalars from endpoint- or derivative-based vectors. Average relations use finite intervals; instantaneous relations use derivatives. Constant-acceleration equations form a narrower conditional view.
end-view
view formula-matter-load-measures | matter-load-measure | mass,weight,mass-density,pressure | formula | Matter and Load Measures | tables/formulas/matter-load-measures.md | These relations distinguish a base quantity from gravitational force and from volume- or area-normalized quantities. Their short algebra is useful only when the subject boundary, environment, averaging scope, and loaded geometry are known.
end-view
view formula-mechanical-work-energy-power | work-energy-power | mechanical-work,energy,kinetic-energy,potential-energy,power | formula | Mechanical Work, Energy, and Power | tables/formulas/mechanical-work-energy-power.md | These relations connect force-displacement transfer, mechanical-energy state terms, and transfer rate. They require an explicit system, path, frame, potential reference, and model scope.
end-view
view formula-newtons-method | unresolved-newtons-method | - | formula | Newton's Method | tables/formulas/newtons-method.md | Newton's method iteratively approximates a root of a differentiable function by intersecting the tangent at the current estimate with the horizontal axis. Its familiar fast convergence is local and conditional, not guaranteed from every starting value.
end-view
view formula-ohms-law | electrical-quantity | electric-current,potential-difference,resistance | formula | Ohm's Law | tables/formulas/ohms-law.md | Ohm's law describes a linear voltage-current relation for an ohmic material or component under the physical conditions for which its resistance remains applicable. Despite its familiar name, it is an empirical relation rather than a universal law of nature.
end-view
view formula-periodic-wave-quantities | periodic-wave-quantity | period,frequency,angular-frequency,wavelength,wave-number,phase,wave-speed | formula | Periodic and Wave Quantities | tables/formulas/periodic-wave-quantities.md | These relations connect temporal recurrence, spatial repetition, phase, and pattern propagation for periodic phenomena and simple traveling waves. Cycle, reference, propagation sign, medium, mode, and regime remain part of the formula contract.
end-view
view formula-probability-range | probability-risk-uncertainty | event-probability | formula | Event Probability Range | tables/formulas/probability-range.md | Within a probability model, every event receives a probability between zero and one inclusive. The bound is a mathematical constraint on a probability measure; it does not make every arbitrary score in that interval a calibrated probability.
end-view
view formula-probability-risk-uncertainty | probability-risk-uncertainty | event-probability,conditional-probability,odds-in-favor,risk,expected-loss,uncertainty,measurement-uncertainty | formula | Probability, Risk, and Uncertainty Relations | tables/formulas/probability-risk-uncertainty.md | These relations connect event probability, odds, consequence-valued expectation, and measurement uncertainty. Risk itself remains a declared model family rather than one universal multiplication rule.
end-view
view formula-pythagorean-trigonometric-identity | unresolved-pythagorean-trigonometric-identity | - | formula | Pythagorean Trigonometric Identity | tables/formulas/pythagorean-trigonometric-identity.md | The Pythagorean trigonometric identity states a relation that holds for every real angle under the standard sine and cosine definitions. It is an identity, not an empirical law and not an equation that selects particular solutions.
end-view
view formula-thermal-quantities | thermal-quantity | temperature,thermal-equilibrium,heat-transfer,internal-energy,heat-capacity,specific-heat-capacity,entropy | formula | Thermal Quantities | tables/formulas/thermal-quantities.md | These relations separate thermal state variables from path-dependent heat and work transfers. They require a system boundary, process conditions, sign convention, phase scope, and absolute temperature where entropy is involved.
